  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ADVANCING BIOACOUSTIC MONITORING AND RESEARCH TO IMPROVE FOREST ELEPHANT CONSERVATION ACROSS POPULATION STRONGHOLDS</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">THE RECENT IUCN RECOGNITION OF THE AFRICAN FOREST ELEPHANT AS A SEPARATE SPECIES FROM THE SAVANNA ELEPHANT, ALONG WITH THE ELEVATION TO CRITICALLY ENDANGERED STATUS, ILLUSTRATES THE URGENT NEED FOR SUSTAINABLE CONSERVATION ACTION TO GUARANTEE THE SURVIVAL OF THIS SPECIES. THERE IS A CRITICAL NEED FOR SYSTEMATIC AND UNBIASED MONITORING TOOLS GENERATING TIMELY OUTPUTS TO FACILITATE ADAPTIVE MANAGEMENT STRATEGIES, AS WELL AS A SUSTAINED COMMITMENT TO BUILDING IN-COUNTRY CAPACITY TO EMPOWER A NEW GENERATION OF CENTRAL AFRICAN CONSERVATIONISTS. SINCE 2007, THE ELEPHANT LISTENING PROJECT (ELP), IN COLLABORATION WITH THE WILDLIFE CONSERVATION SOCIETY (WCS), HAS BEEN PIONEERING THE USE OF PASSIVE ACOUSTIC MONITORING (PAM) TO STUDY FOREST ELEPHANT ECOLOGY AND TO QUANTIFY THE IMPACT OF HUMAN ACTIVITY ON THE SPECIES, ALONG WITH TRAINING TEAMS TO MANAGE SUCH PAM PROJECTS (LARGELY SUPPORTED BY SEVERAL USFWS GRANTS). AS A RESULT, THE ELP AND WCS ESTABLISHED THE SANGHA TRI-NATIONAL CENTER FOR BIOACOUSTICS TRAINING AND ANALYSIS, LOCATED IN NORTHERN REPUBLIC OF CONGO, TO FACILITATE THE USAGE OF PAM TO INFORM ADAPTIVE CONSERVATION MANAGEMENT. LED BY A TEAM OF CONGOLESE EXPERTS, THE CENTER HOSTS WORKSHOPS, ASSISTS WITH BASIC DATA ANALYSIS AND PROVIDES MENTORING FOR CENTRAL AFRICAN STUDENTS. HIGHLIGHTING THE RECOGNIZED NEED FOR PAM</narrative>
  </description>
